---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import { downloads } from '../../config/downloads';
---

<Page
  title="Niyora vs Clue: predict your PMS window and get through it · Niyora"
  description="Clue is a science-minded cycle tracker. It records and predicts, then stops. Niyora predicts your PMS window too, then adds the layer trackers do not have: reflect, regulate, and respond in about a minute, plus training that builds the skill. No account."
  canonicalUrl="https://niyora.com/compare/niyora-vs-clue/"
>
  <section class="s hero">
    <div class="eyebrow">Niyora vs Clue</div>
    <h1>Clue charts your cycle. Niyora steadies you in the moment.</h1>
    <p class="lead">Clue is a careful, science-minded tracker. You log, it charts, it predicts, and there it stops. But a chart does not help you in the hard hour before your period. Niyora tracks and predicts your window too, then adds the layer trackers do not have: one reflection, one regulating action, one response you will not regret, in about a minute, with training that builds the skill before you need it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="s">
    <div class="moat">
      <p>A tracker tells you it is coming, then leaves the hard part to you. Niyora predicts the same window, then <strong>shows up when it lands</strong>: reflect, regulate, respond in about a minute, and training that builds the skill before you need it. A chart is where Clue finishes. For Niyora it is where the help starts.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="s">
    <h2>The difference, line by line</h2>
    <div class="compare-grid">
      <div class="compare-head them">A tracker like Clue</div>
      <div class="compare-head us">Niyora</div>

      <div class="compare-cell them"><p>Records and predicts your cycle with a research-minded eye. Once it predicts, its job is done.</p></div>
      <div class="compare-cell us"><p>Tracks and predicts your window too, then walks you through reflect, regulate, respond when it lands.</p></div>

      <div class="compare-cell them"><p>Prediction is the finish line. You are on your own for the hard part.</p></div>
      <div class="compare-cell us"><p>Training builds the skill before the window, so it lands lighter when it comes.</p></div>

      <div class="compare-cell them"><p>Gives you patterns and predictions from your data.</p></div>
      <div class="compare-cell us"><p>Gives you a way to see the moment differently, settle your body, and respond well.</p></div>

      <div class="compare-cell them"><p>The measure of success is a richer, more accurate record.</p></div>
      <div class="compare-cell us"><p>The measure of success is you moving through it, and needing it less.</p></div>

      <div class="compare-cell them"><p>Spans the whole cycle, built to be used all month.</p></div>
      <div class="compare-cell us"><p>Built for one window. The days before your period.</p></div>

      <div class="compare-cell them"><p>Your logs live in an account so patterns can build over time.</p></div>
      <div class="compare-cell us"><p>No account, no sign in. Your personal details are stripped before anything reaches the AI.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="s">
    <div class="honest">
      <h2>To be fair to Clue</h2>
      <p>Clue does careful, evidence-minded work, and understanding your own pattern has real value. Niyora is not an argument against that. It tracks and predicts too, so this is not tracker versus no tracker. The difference is what happens after the prediction: Clue charts the days, Niyora helps you through them. Many people will want both.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="s">
    <h2>Wondering how hard your premenstrual week really is?</h2>
    <p class="lead">Take the two-minute check and see how your week actually hits.</p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href="/quiz" class="btn">Take the quiz</a>
      <a href={downloads.appStore} class="btn ghost">Get Niyora</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

<style>
  /* Page-specific only: the moat callout, the line-by-line grid, and the
     to-be-fair block. Shared chrome (sheet, sections, dividers, type scale,
     buttons, footer) comes from Page's global CSS. */
  .moat {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 32px 30px;
    border-radius: 20px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(var(--accent-rgb), 0.28);
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.03);
  }
  .moat p { font-size: 21px; font-weight: 300; line-height: 1.5; color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.86); max-width: none; margin: 0; }
  .moat strong { color: rgba(var(--accent-rgb), 0.98); font-weight: 500; }

  .compare-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 14px;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 900px;
    margin: 24px auto 0;
    text-align: left;
  }
  .compare-head { font-size: 12px; letter-spacing: 3px; text-transform: uppercase; padding-bottom: 4px; }
  .compare-head.them { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.4); }
  .compare-head.us { color: rgba(var(--accent-rgb), 0.9); }
  .compare-cell {
    padding: 18px 20px;
    border-radius: 14px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.03);
  }
  .compare-cell p { font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.55; margin: 0; max-width: none; }
  .compare-cell.them { background: rgba(20, 18, 26, 0.4); }
  .compare-cell.them p { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.52); }
  .compare-cell.us { border-color: rgba(var(--accent-rgb), 0.32); }
  .compare-cell.us p { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.86); }

  .honest { max-width: 680px; margin: 0 auto; }
  .honest p { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.72); font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.6; max-width: none; }

  .cta-row { display: flex; gap: 16px; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: center; margin-top: 28px; }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .s { padding: 56px 24px; }
    h1 { font-size: 34px; }
    h2 { font-size: 26px; }
    .moat p { font-size: 18px; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .compare-grid { gap: 10px 12px; }
    .compare-cell { padding: 14px 16px; }
  }
</style>
